e data above.\n\n| $x$ | **$-3$** | **$-1$** | **$0$** | **$4$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| $y$ | $10$ | $8$ | $6$ | $3$ |\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n$\\begin{bmatrix}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}$\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*"] [17592186133536 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(X)\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(β) &= \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 6 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T$, we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and\n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X)^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nβ^(^) &= (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{104}\\begin{bmatrix}26 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}27 \\\\ -26\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}6.75 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\ny = 6.75 - x\n$$"] [17592186133537 :question/problem "An experiment consists of measuring how much a spring stretches (in inches) when one end of the spring is attached to the ceiling while a mass of $x$ ounces hangs from the other end. This data is shown below.\n\n| Mass $(x)$ | **$1$** | **$2$** | **$4$** | **$5$** |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Stretch $(y)$ | $0.8$ | $1.8$ | $3.2$ | $4.2$ |\n\nFind the line of the form $y=\\beta_0 + \\beta_1 x$ that best fits the data above. According to the model, how much will the spring stretch if we use an $8$-ounce mass, assuming that the trend continues?\n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that*\n$\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n*where $X$ is the corresponding design matrix and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.*"] [17592186133537 :question/worked-solution "The coefficients of the line that best fits the data can be found as the least-squares solution ${\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}}$ to the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 4 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(X)\\begin{bmatrix}β_{0} \\\\ β_{1}\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(β) &= \\begin{bmatrix}0.8 \\\\ 1.8 \\\\ 3.2 \\\\ 4.2\\end{bmatrix}^(⏞)^(y)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $X$ is the design matrix, $\\boldsymbol{\\beta}$ is the parameter vector, and $\\mathbf{y}$ is the observation vector.\n\nPre-multiplying this equation by $X^T$, we obtain the corresponding normal equation\n\n$$\nX^T \\! X {\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = X^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSince the columns of $X$ are linearly independent, the matrix ${X^T}X$ is invertible and\n\n$$\n{\\hat{\\boldsymbol{\\beta}}} = (X^T \\! X)^{-1} X^T \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given values of $({X^T} X)^{-1}$ and $X^T \\mathbf{y}$ into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nβ^(^) &= (X^{T}X)^{-1}X^{T}y \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{20}\\begin{bmatrix}23 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 38.2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\\n&= \\begin{bmatrix}0.04 \\\\ 0.82\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the line\n\n$$\ny = 0.04+ 0.82x\n$$\n\nFinally, to predict how much the spring will stretch if an $8$-ounce mass is hung from it, we substitute $x=8$ into the equation of the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\ny &= 0.04 + 0.82(8) \\\\\n&= 6.6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, according to the model, if an $8$ ounce mass is hung on the spring, the spring will stretch $6.6$ inches.\n\n**Note**: Since we're extrapolating ($x=8$ is not within the range of the original set of $x$-values), our result *may* be unreliable."]] "immutable_answers" [17592186130732 17592186130733 17592186130734 17592186130735 17592186130736 17592186130744 17592186130745 17592186130746 17592186130747 17592186130748 17592186130762 17592186130763 17592186130764 17592186130765 17592186130766] "immutable_fields" [[17592186130731 :answer-field/correct] [17592186130731 :answer-field/id] [17592186130731 :answer-field/key] [17592186130731 :answer-field/type] [17592186130743 :answer-field/correct] [17592186130743 :answer-field/id] [17592186130743 :answer-field/key] [17592186130743 :answer-field/type] [17592186130761 :answer-field/correct] [17592186130761 :answer-field/id] [17592186130761 :answer-field/key] [17592186130761 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
